# ...but the web control panel's built client IS committed: it is embedded into
# the terva_web binary via go:embed, so a plain `go build -tags terva_web` (and
# CI / the release) needs no Node.js. Re-include it despite the blanket dist/.
!packages/agent/web/client/dist/
!packages/agent/web/client/dist/**

# Accidentally-committed Go build artifacts. Extension-less Go binaries
# can't be globbed without risking real source, so list the known ones:
# the main binary (`go build ./cmd/terva` without -o drops it at the root;
# prefer `just install` or `-o`), the swarm e2e stub (build it to a TempDir,
# never the tree), and the todo example's compiled output (source only).
/terva
/stubchild
/examples/extensions/todo/terva-todo-extension
# Other cmd/* binaries built without -o also land at the root.
/terva-i18n-lint
/terva-telegram-connector
/terva-discord-connector

node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/

auth.json
sessions/
logs/

*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
.tmp/
tmp/
npm/

# Harness-generated review/eval reports dropped at the repo root. They
# are transient working artifacts; keeping them untracked stops one from
# slipping into a commit and leaking into the public release tree (the
# release overlay strips docs/plans/, not stray root files). Move a
# report into docs/ deliberately if it's worth keeping.
/*-report.md

# Agent worktrees: the coding-agent harness creates isolated git worktrees
# here for background tasks and removes them when done. Ignore so an
# in-progress agent run never dirties the tree (or the build's version stamp).
.claude/worktrees/
